8 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552398 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552398 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552398 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552398 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fcd63b2c-12c1-54cb-802c-3c21ad3aab91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552411 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18af1f73-e41b-5d4c-86dd-e0a8cf91a4f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552411 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same  elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552422 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "262002ef-4fd0-5419-8127-789c5234e1d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552422 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of the identity matrix and adding $-9$ times the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-9{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552432 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46fec928-7e8c-5465-b67b-b3ef68641ebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552432 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the  three given elementary row operations. \n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552440 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\,$ to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59ac31ae-d95c-5ade-b172-6fbd672350bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552440 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}$  to $A.$ \n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552443 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "608d80e2-812d-50df-95f4-906e16812c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552443 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}.$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552450 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "697a3132-99c3-5286-b927-185afe5caf9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552450 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of the identity matrix and adding $6$ times the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552453 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f2a6c54-ca8b-593a-b61e-5ecaccecd8e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552453 #edb/ref 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is 